WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:37:14.522 --> 00:37:22.330
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:37:22.330 --> 00:37:24.550
ALEX BAIN: Welcome
to Cloud OnAir,

00:37:24.550 --> 00:37:26.470
live webinars from Google Cloud.

00:37:26.470 --> 00:37:28.536
We are hosting
webinars every Tuesday.

00:37:28.536 --> 00:37:29.410
My name is Alex Bain.

00:37:29.410 --> 00:37:31.720
I'm the head of marketing
analytics at Nest.

00:37:31.720 --> 00:37:34.319
I'm here with Jesse Nichols,
the head of growth at Nest.

00:37:34.319 --> 00:37:35.860
And today, we'll be
talking about how

00:37:35.860 --> 00:37:39.190
Nest uses Google Cloud and
Tableau to drive its business.

00:37:39.190 --> 00:37:41.470
You can ask questions anytime
on the platform, where

00:37:41.470 --> 00:37:44.540
we have Googlers on
standby to answer them.

00:37:44.540 --> 00:37:46.615
Let's get started.

00:37:46.615 --> 00:37:47.490
JESSE NICHOLS: Great.

00:37:47.490 --> 00:37:49.620
So first of all, since
Alex is a marketer,

00:37:49.620 --> 00:37:52.140
and I run growth primarily
from the standpoint of growth

00:37:52.140 --> 00:37:54.420
analytics experimentation,
this presentation

00:37:54.420 --> 00:37:57.300
will be how we drive the
business through that lens.

00:37:57.300 --> 00:38:00.410
We use Google Analytics to drive
a huge portion of our marketing

00:38:00.410 --> 00:38:03.919
analysis, and since
we use Analytics 360,

00:38:03.919 --> 00:38:05.460
we also leverage
the integration that

00:38:05.460 --> 00:38:08.040
sends all of your
website analytics data

00:38:08.040 --> 00:38:11.520
into Google BigQuery, of
course, within the cloud.

00:38:11.520 --> 00:38:13.470
So to begin, we'll
talk, first, about why

00:38:13.470 --> 00:38:15.780
you would use Tableau
versus just using

00:38:15.780 --> 00:38:18.490
Google Analytics interface
to do marketing analytics,

00:38:18.490 --> 00:38:21.210
or how you could get
extra insights from that.

00:38:21.210 --> 00:38:22.980
Then we'll talk
more about why you

00:38:22.980 --> 00:38:25.500
would connect through BigQuery
versus the Google Analytics

00:38:25.500 --> 00:38:29.470
API, which Tableau also has
a native integration with.

00:38:29.470 --> 00:38:31.594
And finally, we'll end
with some tips and tricks

00:38:31.594 --> 00:38:32.760
to get you off to the races.

00:38:35.639 --> 00:38:36.430
ALEX BAIN: Perfect.

00:38:36.430 --> 00:38:41.380
So let's talk about Tableau
versus the GA interface.

00:38:41.380 --> 00:38:44.260
So both tools are capable
of segmenting users

00:38:44.260 --> 00:38:45.874
and visualizing data.

00:38:45.874 --> 00:38:47.290
So how do you
decide when you want

00:38:47.290 --> 00:38:51.340
to use one versus the other?

00:38:51.340 --> 00:38:54.600
So for Google Analytics,
channel reporting

00:38:54.600 --> 00:38:56.380
is very quick and easy.

00:38:56.380 --> 00:38:59.040
If you would just want
to understand sessions

00:38:59.040 --> 00:39:01.110
from a specific
channel like display,

00:39:01.110 --> 00:39:04.830
you can get that
very fast in GA.

00:39:04.830 --> 00:39:08.760
Additionally, AdWords,
so shopping ads,

00:39:08.760 --> 00:39:11.750
YouTube DoubleClick,
search console data--

00:39:11.750 --> 00:39:13.830
a handful of these
dimensions and metrics

00:39:13.830 --> 00:39:15.780
simply aren't available
through the API,

00:39:15.780 --> 00:39:18.820
and GA is a great
resource in those cases.

00:39:18.820 --> 00:39:22.440
And then, finally, simple
answers to simple questions--

00:39:22.440 --> 00:39:24.330
so sometimes you
just need to grab

00:39:24.330 --> 00:39:27.270
one metric, a one-metric
answer to a question,

00:39:27.270 --> 00:39:31.870
and no reason to go
beyond GA in those cases.

00:39:31.870 --> 00:39:33.690
So channel reporting--
for example,

00:39:33.690 --> 00:39:38.160
sometimes you get a request
about a specific flighted

00:39:38.160 --> 00:39:40.260
campaign and how it performed.

00:39:40.260 --> 00:39:43.590
It's just easier to get that
in GA if it's a click away,

00:39:43.590 --> 00:39:46.680
and you can bookmark that, send
it out to stakeholders as well.

00:39:49.790 --> 00:39:52.335
So then when do you use Tableau?

00:39:52.335 --> 00:39:54.460
So if you have multiple
data sources-- for example,

00:39:54.460 --> 00:39:57.020
you can flow in the
product hierarchy.

00:39:57.020 --> 00:40:00.040
So if you want to group a
number of SKUs a certain way,

00:40:00.040 --> 00:40:03.660
very easy to create
that reference table

00:40:03.660 --> 00:40:07.210
and change it
dynamically as needed,

00:40:07.210 --> 00:40:08.950
as new products
come out, and have

00:40:08.950 --> 00:40:13.510
Tableau sort of pointed there
and grouping data that way.

00:40:13.510 --> 00:40:18.760
So pre-click marketing data--
anything you're doing offline--

00:40:18.760 --> 00:40:22.990
for example, you can create
that as another data source

00:40:22.990 --> 00:40:27.250
that you would fold in
alongside all of the GA data

00:40:27.250 --> 00:40:29.500
that you have flowing in.

00:40:29.500 --> 00:40:31.510
Custom dashboards--
so dashboards

00:40:31.510 --> 00:40:34.660
are great for tracking campaign
or channel performance,

00:40:34.660 --> 00:40:36.910
and marketers love
pretty dashboards,

00:40:36.910 --> 00:40:40.240
which Tableau makes
it very easy to build.

00:40:40.240 --> 00:40:42.550
So if you want to
create multiple views

00:40:42.550 --> 00:40:45.100
of multiple metrics
all in one place,

00:40:45.100 --> 00:40:47.290
very easy to do that in Tableau.

00:40:47.290 --> 00:40:49.820
And then, finally,
performance versus goals,

00:40:49.820 --> 00:40:52.120
so if you establish goals at
the beginning of the year,

00:40:52.120 --> 00:40:54.520
and you have those
documented somewhere--

00:40:54.520 --> 00:40:56.110
and you see that
in the visual here,

00:40:56.110 --> 00:40:58.780
where you have kind of gray
bars referencing the goals--

00:40:58.780 --> 00:41:01.840
then you can flow in the
actuals through BigQuery

00:41:01.840 --> 00:41:04.090
into Tableau each
week that compares

00:41:04.090 --> 00:41:06.610
how you did against your goals.

00:41:06.610 --> 00:41:09.790
Very, very helpful as a kind
of ongoing reference in,

00:41:09.790 --> 00:41:12.852
say, like a weekly
stakeholder meeting.

00:41:12.852 --> 00:41:13.810
JESSE NICHOLS: Exactly.

00:41:13.810 --> 00:41:17.770
And similarly, when you're
doing site analysis or growth

00:41:17.770 --> 00:41:20.200
optimization analysis,
Google Analytics

00:41:20.200 --> 00:41:24.760
is a fantastic tool for just
digging around and exploring

00:41:24.760 --> 00:41:25.480
the data.

00:41:25.480 --> 00:41:27.580
I mean, first and
foremost, when someone

00:41:27.580 --> 00:41:30.130
is asking a straightforward
question, a single answer

00:41:30.130 --> 00:41:32.710
question, it's just way easier
to go through the interface

00:41:32.710 --> 00:41:34.670
and find it in
the canned report.

00:41:34.670 --> 00:41:36.970
So if someone's asking,
how did this one URL

00:41:36.970 --> 00:41:40.060
do from a temporary
campaign we just launched,

00:41:40.060 --> 00:41:42.670
or what's the percent
of mobile traffic,

00:41:42.670 --> 00:41:45.100
or what's the
percent of EU traffic

00:41:45.100 --> 00:41:47.750
from this specific country,
a lot of that analysis--

00:41:47.750 --> 00:41:48.760
it's a single answer.

00:41:48.760 --> 00:41:50.301
Not going to have
to go get it again.

00:41:50.301 --> 00:41:53.020
Find it in GA, give
it, and move on.

00:41:53.020 --> 00:41:55.870
Again, even when the
data consumer is myself,

00:41:55.870 --> 00:41:58.930
it's often a lot easier to
start an exploratory analysis

00:41:58.930 --> 00:42:01.660
from within Google Analytics.

00:42:01.660 --> 00:42:03.954
There was a recent
issue where I was

00:42:03.954 --> 00:42:06.370
trying to find if there were
any browser versions affected

00:42:06.370 --> 00:42:09.484
by a site update,
technology update,

00:42:09.484 --> 00:42:11.650
and it was just easier to
dig through the interface,

00:42:11.650 --> 00:42:13.630
find out what, if
anything, was at risk,

00:42:13.630 --> 00:42:15.704
and give an answer to that.

00:42:15.704 --> 00:42:18.370
Finally, the reason that I would
use Google Analytics as opposed

00:42:18.370 --> 00:42:22.900
to Tableau for analysis is
this kind of segmentation tool

00:42:22.900 --> 00:42:26.206
that GA provides, where it's
basically a WYSIWYG Query

00:42:26.206 --> 00:42:27.580
Builder, and this
concept's going

00:42:27.580 --> 00:42:30.760
to come back in full force
when we talk about BigQuery.

00:42:30.760 --> 00:42:34.480
But this tool that you see
in the screenshot there

00:42:34.480 --> 00:42:36.160
allows you to kind
of functionally

00:42:36.160 --> 00:42:38.890
put together the rudimentary
basics of a query

00:42:38.890 --> 00:42:40.720
and get a single
answer, a single metric

00:42:40.720 --> 00:42:42.310
to a single question.

00:42:42.310 --> 00:42:43.820
Even if you don't
save that segment,

00:42:43.820 --> 00:42:44.861
it's a pretty handy tool.

00:42:44.861 --> 00:42:46.480
But it's got its limits.

00:42:46.480 --> 00:42:50.200
Now in Tableau, I totally agree.

00:42:50.200 --> 00:42:52.000
Fully custom
dashboards-- you just

00:42:52.000 --> 00:42:54.440
can't go wrong with what
you can do with Tableau.

00:42:54.440 --> 00:42:59.304
I mean you can go plenty wrong,
but usually, the handcuffs

00:42:59.304 --> 00:43:00.970
are off to a large
degree in what you're

00:43:00.970 --> 00:43:03.190
trying to design and build.

00:43:03.190 --> 00:43:05.920
The GA dashboards
are quite limited.

00:43:05.920 --> 00:43:07.600
They're quite constricting.

00:43:07.600 --> 00:43:10.251
But with Tableau, you've
got design colors.

00:43:10.251 --> 00:43:12.250
You're really putting
together a full dashboard.

00:43:12.250 --> 00:43:15.280
So I love that I can have my
marketers-- give my marketers

00:43:15.280 --> 00:43:17.530
something that they can
self-serve with and anticipate

00:43:17.530 --> 00:43:18.374
their needs.

00:43:18.374 --> 00:43:20.290
And then there's a handful
of features, again,

00:43:20.290 --> 00:43:22.392
that you can do inside
the GA interface--

00:43:22.392 --> 00:43:24.100
just a lot easier when
you're in Tableau,

00:43:24.100 --> 00:43:26.432
such as providing filters
to limit the amount of data

00:43:26.432 --> 00:43:28.390
or change the data that's
someone seeing within

00:43:28.390 --> 00:43:31.150
a dashboard, and even
controlling exactly which

00:43:31.150 --> 00:43:34.080
filters you get, which filters
you're-- which dimensions

00:43:34.080 --> 00:43:35.290
you're filtering on.

00:43:35.290 --> 00:43:37.810
So that kind of stuff, again,
way better through Tableau.

00:43:37.810 --> 00:43:39.880
And then, finally, again,
these are technologies

00:43:39.880 --> 00:43:41.890
that we leverage in
great detail when

00:43:41.890 --> 00:43:43.910
we start to flow
in BigQuery data,

00:43:43.910 --> 00:43:47.290
but even just your
normal Tableau interface

00:43:47.290 --> 00:43:51.130
is far better at slicing your
data by a certain dimension

00:43:51.130 --> 00:43:54.790
or helping you differentiate
between line or bar charts

00:43:54.790 --> 00:43:58.400
through coloring and other
classic data vis techniques.

00:43:58.400 --> 00:44:01.150
So I think that it's
very situational,

00:44:01.150 --> 00:44:03.300
but Tableau gives us a
lot more flexibility when

00:44:03.300 --> 00:44:04.591
we're trying to share out data.

00:44:06.990 --> 00:44:09.820
OK, so once you've kind of got
a sense of when you would use

00:44:09.820 --> 00:44:12.980
Tableau over GA, it begs
the question of, well,

00:44:12.980 --> 00:44:15.610
why wouldn't you just be using
the Google Analytics API, which

00:44:15.610 --> 00:44:17.390
is already within Tableau?

00:44:17.390 --> 00:44:20.580
What's the extra benefit
you get through BigQuery?

00:44:20.580 --> 00:44:22.330
ALEX BAIN: Sure.

00:44:22.330 --> 00:44:24.370
So both are just a click away.

00:44:24.370 --> 00:44:26.500
As you can see,
individuals here,

00:44:26.500 --> 00:44:28.450
as you're setting
up a new Tableau

00:44:28.450 --> 00:44:33.790
file-- very easy to pull in
GA directly through that API

00:44:33.790 --> 00:44:37.270
or BigQuery directly
through that API.

00:44:37.270 --> 00:44:41.050
But if you go the
GA route, and you're

00:44:41.050 --> 00:44:43.750
working with a relatively
large data set,

00:44:43.750 --> 00:44:46.450
you can be waiting a long time.

00:44:46.450 --> 00:44:49.120
So BigQuery comes
back about as fast

00:44:49.120 --> 00:44:51.950
as the cloud can get it to you.

00:44:51.950 --> 00:44:56.200
So for example, if you're
looking at multi-year data

00:44:56.200 --> 00:45:00.790
sets, and you're trying to
understand seasonality trends,

00:45:00.790 --> 00:45:04.510
you're going to get into
sample data on the--

00:45:04.510 --> 00:45:07.750
if you're using
the GA interface,

00:45:07.750 --> 00:45:09.790
and you won't know
as you're looking

00:45:09.790 --> 00:45:12.610
at noise if that is
due to seasonality

00:45:12.610 --> 00:45:14.500
or if that is due to sampling.

00:45:14.500 --> 00:45:17.610
So the BigQuery interface or
API helps a bunch in that case.

00:45:17.610 --> 00:45:20.110
JESSE NICHOLS: Yeah, I mean I
don't know how many times I've

00:45:20.110 --> 00:45:22.540
been asked about some
very small metric that

00:45:22.540 --> 00:45:24.310
is very important,
very business critical,

00:45:24.310 --> 00:45:25.970
but it's like a
needle in a haystack.

00:45:25.970 --> 00:45:30.700
And even with robust sampling,
you just don't even--

00:45:30.700 --> 00:45:32.610
data's almost not usable
at a certain level,

00:45:32.610 --> 00:45:34.290
and certainly as
you go back in time.

00:45:34.290 --> 00:45:36.490
So you know I've been
involved in a lot of analysis

00:45:36.490 --> 00:45:39.240
projects, where precision
is just necessary.

00:45:39.240 --> 00:45:42.490
And BigQuery, you just have no
reason to give up precision.

00:45:42.490 --> 00:45:43.156
ALEX BAIN: Yeah.

00:45:45.871 --> 00:45:46.370
Jesse.

00:45:46.370 --> 00:45:50.210
JESSE NICHOLS: So you know,
I think, as an analyst,

00:45:50.210 --> 00:45:52.490
certainly as someone
who's spent probably

00:45:52.490 --> 00:45:54.530
80%, 90% of my time
in analytics tools,

00:45:54.530 --> 00:45:58.160
having unlimited control
over the segmentation you're

00:45:58.160 --> 00:46:00.770
doing within the data
is incredibly powerful.

00:46:00.770 --> 00:46:02.514
And it's also
incredibly frustrating

00:46:02.514 --> 00:46:04.180
when you know the
answer is in the data,

00:46:04.180 --> 00:46:06.600
and you just can't get to it.

00:46:06.600 --> 00:46:11.300
So the limit here is that
within Tableau's GA connection,

00:46:11.300 --> 00:46:14.420
you are limited, basically,
to GA's segment builder.

00:46:14.420 --> 00:46:16.310
You have to use the
segmentation builder

00:46:16.310 --> 00:46:18.130
that I showed before
in GA's interface

00:46:18.130 --> 00:46:19.795
before you port it
over to Tableau.

00:46:19.795 --> 00:46:21.920
And there are a whole bunch
of things that you just

00:46:21.920 --> 00:46:25.010
can't build in that thing that
just feel just out of reach.

00:46:25.010 --> 00:46:26.910
Specifically, when
you have, let's say,

00:46:26.910 --> 00:46:29.210
a pretty standardized
event naming structure--

00:46:29.210 --> 00:46:31.760
the same event could fire
from multiple pages--

00:46:31.760 --> 00:46:33.710
you can't really tie
those two things together

00:46:33.710 --> 00:46:35.480
in the GA segment builder.

00:46:35.480 --> 00:46:39.230
But as an e-commerce shop,
just knowing certain product

00:46:39.230 --> 00:46:41.760
combinations being
purchased together, again,

00:46:41.760 --> 00:46:44.000
just one of the limits of
the GA segment builder.

00:46:44.000 --> 00:46:47.960
You can't build these kind
of multi-product combinations

00:46:47.960 --> 00:46:51.577
or adding multiple products
to your cart over one journey.

00:46:51.577 --> 00:46:53.660
You can really only analyze
one product at a time,

00:46:53.660 --> 00:46:56.750
so it's kind of a showstopper
for a company like Nest.

00:46:56.750 --> 00:46:58.790
But really, pretty much
any hit level logic,

00:46:58.790 --> 00:47:02.150
you're constrained from doing
in the GA segment builder,

00:47:02.150 --> 00:47:05.150
while, in BigQuery, you
can see I shamefully

00:47:05.150 --> 00:47:11.000
used legacy SQL to flag segments
with pretty much any segment

00:47:11.000 --> 00:47:12.710
logic that I want.

00:47:12.710 --> 00:47:18.350
And the idea is that that logic
that you're putting in there

00:47:18.350 --> 00:47:21.457
is all hit level, and if you
need to reconcile segments that

00:47:21.457 --> 00:47:23.540
have-- sessions that have
multiple hits within one

00:47:23.540 --> 00:47:27.560
session, then you just
stack a bunch of these,

00:47:27.560 --> 00:47:29.055
and use and/or
rules, just like you

00:47:29.055 --> 00:47:31.460
would in the segment builder
in some sort of outer query,

00:47:31.460 --> 00:47:31.970
and boom.

00:47:31.970 --> 00:47:34.700
You have any segment
you could want to build.

00:47:34.700 --> 00:47:37.340
You can investigate any set
of users that you'd want.

00:47:37.340 --> 00:47:40.160
It was really a freeing
moment, as an analyst, when

00:47:40.160 --> 00:47:42.620
I finally put all these
pieces together and was

00:47:42.620 --> 00:47:45.050
like, oh, OK, yeah, game on.

00:47:45.050 --> 00:47:47.720
It was a nice moment, so I
strongly encourage digging

00:47:47.720 --> 00:47:52.630
into this if you haven't used
the GA data in BigQuery before.

00:47:52.630 --> 00:47:56.210
The thing that you can
stack on from there,

00:47:56.210 --> 00:47:59.940
however, is, I think, an order
of magnitude more powerful.

00:47:59.940 --> 00:48:03.050
So within-- again,
as an analyst,

00:48:03.050 --> 00:48:05.780
you're trying to find
business insights that

00:48:05.780 --> 00:48:09.016
are relative to
their total scale

00:48:09.016 --> 00:48:11.390
or relative to the context of
the greater business, which

00:48:11.390 --> 00:48:13.400
is really about a
calculated metric.

00:48:13.400 --> 00:48:14.704
A flat standard metric--

00:48:14.704 --> 00:48:17.120
it's kind of like, is that a
number that's-- is that a big

00:48:17.120 --> 00:48:17.619
number?

00:48:17.619 --> 00:48:19.085
Is it a large
percent of our sales?

00:48:19.085 --> 00:48:20.960
And so these calculated
metrics are critical.

00:48:20.960 --> 00:48:22.750
They're just absolutely
vital to understanding--

00:48:22.750 --> 00:48:23.960
these ratios are
vital to understanding

00:48:23.960 --> 00:48:26.251
the health of the business
or the health of some metric

00:48:26.251 --> 00:48:27.140
you're pursuing.

00:48:27.140 --> 00:48:30.830
And the constraint with
both GA's segment builder

00:48:30.830 --> 00:48:33.440
and the Tableau
connection to GA is

00:48:33.440 --> 00:48:37.645
that you can segment
some session metric,

00:48:37.645 --> 00:48:39.770
and then you can segment
some other session metric.

00:48:39.770 --> 00:48:42.390
But because these are
different segments,

00:48:42.390 --> 00:48:44.509
the numbers have no
relationship to each other.

00:48:44.509 --> 00:48:46.300
You can't just divide
one on top of another

00:48:46.300 --> 00:48:48.320
to create a new
calculated metric,

00:48:48.320 --> 00:48:50.260
because, again, that
segment is applied

00:48:50.260 --> 00:48:52.100
to all data in the structure.

00:48:52.100 --> 00:48:54.800
And you're just
kind of hamstrung,

00:48:54.800 --> 00:48:57.470
and you got to do a lot of
work to torture that out

00:48:57.470 --> 00:48:59.360
of the tool, and
then recombine it.

00:48:59.360 --> 00:49:00.590
It's just a real pain.

00:49:00.590 --> 00:49:03.291
But when you do this
work within BigQuery,

00:49:03.291 --> 00:49:05.540
like I showed you before,
your segments-- you can kind

00:49:05.540 --> 00:49:06.900
of capture however you want.

00:49:06.900 --> 00:49:10.250
And you bubble them
up as a standard kind

00:49:10.250 --> 00:49:12.110
of pre-calced metric.

00:49:12.110 --> 00:49:14.180
And then you've got
yourself a numerator

00:49:14.180 --> 00:49:17.750
and denominator and, again,
unlimited total control.

00:49:17.750 --> 00:49:21.230
If you were trying to
seat yourself to GA,

00:49:21.230 --> 00:49:24.200
you'd have to have all this
foresight to set a goal,

00:49:24.200 --> 00:49:26.050
and you only get a
certain number of goals.

00:49:26.050 --> 00:49:27.508
Even if you set a
goal, it can only

00:49:27.508 --> 00:49:30.050
be applied in the future
and not retroactively.

00:49:30.050 --> 00:49:31.510
This unlocks all of it.

00:49:31.510 --> 00:49:33.622
You just kind of--
the stress is gone.

00:49:33.622 --> 00:49:35.330
ALEX BAIN: Maybe we
should do the example

00:49:35.330 --> 00:49:37.040
of shoppers on nest.com.

00:49:37.040 --> 00:49:38.420
That was a good insight you had.

00:49:38.420 --> 00:49:39.470
JESSE NICHOLS: Yeah,
that was another kind

00:49:39.470 --> 00:49:40.490
of game-changing
moment in the way

00:49:40.490 --> 00:49:42.680
that we did analysis and looked
for growth opportunities.

00:49:42.680 --> 00:49:45.096
You know, nest.com-- you can
do all sorts of things there.

00:49:45.096 --> 00:49:48.030
You can manage your--
control your devices.

00:49:48.030 --> 00:49:49.040
You can get support.

00:49:49.040 --> 00:49:51.470
You can work with pro partners.

00:49:51.470 --> 00:49:54.740
So only a limited segment of
our total visit population

00:49:54.740 --> 00:49:56.030
are these shoppers.

00:49:56.030 --> 00:49:58.220
And, in fact, if you
had a big support issue,

00:49:58.220 --> 00:50:01.112
you'd have a burst in
traffic and a decline

00:50:01.112 --> 00:50:03.320
in conversion rate, when,
really, your shopper metric

00:50:03.320 --> 00:50:04.544
was totally unaffected.

00:50:04.544 --> 00:50:05.960
And so we really
had to figure out

00:50:05.960 --> 00:50:08.720
a way to not only pull those
shoppers out as a segment,

00:50:08.720 --> 00:50:12.756
but then, also, that needed to
become the de facto denominator

00:50:12.756 --> 00:50:13.880
in all of our calculations.

00:50:13.880 --> 00:50:15.130
And anything you
stack on top of it

00:50:15.130 --> 00:50:16.800
kind of had to take
that as a given.

00:50:16.800 --> 00:50:19.310
So we defined find a
shopper and, again, started

00:50:19.310 --> 00:50:20.570
building metrics on top of it.

00:50:20.570 --> 00:50:21.812
How many shoppers were--

00:50:21.812 --> 00:50:23.270
what percent of
shoppers were going

00:50:23.270 --> 00:50:24.470
through this specific flow?

00:50:24.470 --> 00:50:27.470
What percent of shoppers were
buying a mixed transaction

00:50:27.470 --> 00:50:30.430
with more than one piece
of core hardware in it?

00:50:30.430 --> 00:50:33.069
And again, these calcs had just
been out of our reach before.

00:50:33.069 --> 00:50:34.610
So this was a real
game changer using

00:50:34.610 --> 00:50:36.620
BigQuery to mash
all this together

00:50:36.620 --> 00:50:38.122
and Tableau to visualize.

00:50:42.190 --> 00:50:45.010
And then, finally, just
to ice this cake a bit,

00:50:45.010 --> 00:50:48.340
I love to take the
segmented metrics

00:50:48.340 --> 00:50:51.882
that I've built in BigQuery
and turn them into dimensions

00:50:51.882 --> 00:50:52.840
using a case statement.

00:50:52.840 --> 00:50:55.140
And I think you can probably--
you could use these case

00:50:55.140 --> 00:50:56.990
statements in either
Tableau or BigQuery,

00:50:56.990 --> 00:50:59.290
but it is still the fundamental
work, the groundwork

00:50:59.290 --> 00:51:02.020
that you've laid
that enables this.

00:51:02.020 --> 00:51:04.360
And what this is
referring to is,

00:51:04.360 --> 00:51:06.460
usually, you're stuck
with the dimensions

00:51:06.460 --> 00:51:08.290
that you've had to do
your kind of slices

00:51:08.290 --> 00:51:12.310
of comparisons-- country,
device category, channel,

00:51:12.310 --> 00:51:13.460
things like that.

00:51:13.460 --> 00:51:15.880
But really, when
you want to know

00:51:15.880 --> 00:51:19.846
the impact of using a thing
versus not using a thing,

00:51:19.846 --> 00:51:21.220
if you want to be
able to compare

00:51:21.220 --> 00:51:23.470
that fluidly in
Tableau, you need

00:51:23.470 --> 00:51:25.137
to be able to treat
that as a dimension.

00:51:25.137 --> 00:51:27.720
And so using a case statement,
you take these underlying rules

00:51:27.720 --> 00:51:29.140
that you've built,
and you simply

00:51:29.140 --> 00:51:32.896
say, OK, well if they did this
thing, you name them one thing.

00:51:32.896 --> 00:51:35.020
If they didn't do it, you
name them something else.

00:51:35.020 --> 00:51:37.980
And boom, you can do this
concept that, at least at Nest,

00:51:37.980 --> 00:51:40.720
we refer to by slicing
by or coloring by,

00:51:40.720 --> 00:51:43.030
to take all of the metrics
you've built below,

00:51:43.030 --> 00:51:45.430
all the calculated metrics,
and simply chop them

00:51:45.430 --> 00:51:48.220
by the dimension
you just outlined.

00:51:48.220 --> 00:51:52.390
So in this false
example, if we had a way

00:51:52.390 --> 00:51:57.031
to infer what someone's
dietary preference was based

00:51:57.031 --> 00:51:58.780
on some series of
actions they could take,

00:51:58.780 --> 00:52:01.390
we can build those as metrics
that would flag those sessions.

00:52:01.390 --> 00:52:03.265
And then you use case
statements to just kind

00:52:03.265 --> 00:52:05.140
of separate each
group into each other.

00:52:05.140 --> 00:52:07.030
And then all of the
conversion rate metrics,

00:52:07.030 --> 00:52:09.160
segmentation traffic
metrics, everything

00:52:09.160 --> 00:52:11.355
you kind of make
beneath that can now

00:52:11.355 --> 00:52:14.140
be sliced by this new
magical inference dimension

00:52:14.140 --> 00:52:16.300
that you've pulled out of.

00:52:16.300 --> 00:52:18.926
So pretty great stuff, and,
again, huge supercharge

00:52:18.926 --> 00:52:20.050
to the analysis we've done.

00:52:20.050 --> 00:52:22.049
ALEX BAIN: Stakeholders
feel very empowered when

00:52:22.049 --> 00:52:24.280
they can play around
with the interface

00:52:24.280 --> 00:52:26.680
to make it reflect
what they want to see.

00:52:26.680 --> 00:52:29.860
It also is the case, then, that
if it all lives in one vis,

00:52:29.860 --> 00:52:31.990
that if you need to
make formatting changes

00:52:31.990 --> 00:52:34.990
or incorporate new
data sources later on,

00:52:34.990 --> 00:52:36.750
you don't have to
do that times five.

00:52:36.750 --> 00:52:39.310
If you've got that built into
the slice by or color by,

00:52:39.310 --> 00:52:40.520
it's all in one place.

00:52:40.520 --> 00:52:41.140
JESSE NICHOLS: Yeah,
and, again, this

00:52:41.140 --> 00:52:42.598
isn't really the
same thing as just

00:52:42.598 --> 00:52:45.310
using grouping functionality
on normal GA data.

00:52:45.310 --> 00:52:48.070
You actually need that data
to come through BigQuery

00:52:48.070 --> 00:52:52.079
to be where you're building
metrics off of the segments

00:52:52.079 --> 00:52:52.870
in the first place.

00:52:52.870 --> 00:52:54.100
And then once you've
done that, you

00:52:54.100 --> 00:52:55.933
can really transform
your utility with this.

00:53:00.581 --> 00:53:02.080
Well, hopefully,
we can convince you

00:53:02.080 --> 00:53:04.560
at least a little bit
that using BigQuery

00:53:04.560 --> 00:53:07.020
can give you far more power
when looking for marketing

00:53:07.020 --> 00:53:08.460
and growth opportunities.

00:53:08.460 --> 00:53:10.020
Now we'll share a
few tips and tricks

00:53:10.020 --> 00:53:13.844
on how we use traditional
Tableau capabilities layered

00:53:13.844 --> 00:53:15.510
on top of this stuff
that we've unlocked

00:53:15.510 --> 00:53:17.940
with BigQuery to,
again, really power

00:53:17.940 --> 00:53:23.000
up our marketing and
vis opportunities.

00:53:23.000 --> 00:53:25.990
ALEX BAIN: So when you're
evaluating marketing

00:53:25.990 --> 00:53:28.450
performance, it's very
straightforward to divide

00:53:28.450 --> 00:53:33.940
revenue and cost, and call that
RoAS, or Return on Ad Spend.

00:53:33.940 --> 00:53:36.070
The challenge is if
you're selling something

00:53:36.070 --> 00:53:39.610
that itself has cost, a cost
of goods sold, then by the time

00:53:39.610 --> 00:53:42.130
you've pulled that
out, what you thought

00:53:42.130 --> 00:53:45.620
was break-even is actually
quite unprofitable.

00:53:45.620 --> 00:53:49.090
So what you really want to do
is look at margin or profit

00:53:49.090 --> 00:53:51.070
divided by cost.

00:53:51.070 --> 00:53:53.470
So if you have the
concept for a given,

00:53:53.470 --> 00:53:57.680
say, hardware product of what
it costs to make that product,

00:53:57.680 --> 00:54:01.570
you can create a reference
table that has the price as well

00:54:01.570 --> 00:54:04.420
as the, say, profit per unit.

00:54:04.420 --> 00:54:06.430
And you can evaluate
what you're spending

00:54:06.430 --> 00:54:08.230
on marketing relative
to the profit

00:54:08.230 --> 00:54:10.210
that you're bringing
into the company,

00:54:10.210 --> 00:54:13.670
and see if your marketing
is truly paying for itself.

00:54:13.670 --> 00:54:16.347
When it is, it's very easy
to secure additional budget.

00:54:16.347 --> 00:54:18.430
JESSE NICHOLS: Yeah, and
it's a lot nicer this way

00:54:18.430 --> 00:54:20.170
than to ask your
finance team if you

00:54:20.170 --> 00:54:22.290
can put your margin into
a custom metric that

00:54:22.290 --> 00:54:23.770
flows over the open web.

00:54:23.770 --> 00:54:26.740
So it's an even kind of
upgrade, even from, I think,

00:54:26.740 --> 00:54:29.710
what normal GA
capabilities would provide.

00:54:29.710 --> 00:54:31.120
ALEX BAIN: So another example--

00:54:31.120 --> 00:54:32.830
agencies can keep
their secret sauce.

00:54:32.830 --> 00:54:35.890
So if your agency is
going out and negotiating

00:54:35.890 --> 00:54:39.940
preferential costs with
individual platforms,

00:54:39.940 --> 00:54:42.580
they may not want to share that,
or it might be in the contract

00:54:42.580 --> 00:54:44.290
that it can't be shared.

00:54:44.290 --> 00:54:47.590
So they could create, for
example, an aggregated view

00:54:47.590 --> 00:54:50.830
of their cost, where it's
aggregated at a level

00:54:50.830 --> 00:54:53.716
that it's consistent
with their contract.

00:54:53.716 --> 00:54:55.090
They could put
that, for example,

00:54:55.090 --> 00:54:57.850
into Google Sheets, which
could be pointed to Tableau

00:54:57.850 --> 00:54:59.590
as an additional data source.

00:54:59.590 --> 00:55:02.590
So you can look at traffic,
conversion, et cetera,

00:55:02.590 --> 00:55:04.540
and you can do it in
the context of the cost

00:55:04.540 --> 00:55:07.210
that the agency is
reporting to you.

00:55:07.210 --> 00:55:10.150
So it makes it easy
to be compliant.

00:55:10.150 --> 00:55:12.680
Additionally, orders
versus shipments--

00:55:12.680 --> 00:55:15.520
so it might be
the case that what

00:55:15.520 --> 00:55:18.370
you're seeing in GA is orders,
but if you were actually

00:55:18.370 --> 00:55:21.190
shipping something,
if you had preorders,

00:55:21.190 --> 00:55:23.860
if you had a backlog,
if there's just

00:55:23.860 --> 00:55:26.240
some sort of delay
with a supplier,

00:55:26.240 --> 00:55:28.240
there can be a gap between
orders and shipments,

00:55:28.240 --> 00:55:33.190
and Tableau helps you to blend
that data in and visualize

00:55:33.190 --> 00:55:34.620
the difference.

00:55:34.620 --> 00:55:38.940
It can be very handy, especially
at peak times of the year.

00:55:38.940 --> 00:55:43.840
Parameterizing dimensions--
sounds fancy, because it is.

00:55:43.840 --> 00:55:46.300
So it's a classic
Tableau feature.

00:55:46.300 --> 00:55:48.790
It becomes even more
powerful if you have segments

00:55:48.790 --> 00:55:50.000
that you're working with.

00:55:50.000 --> 00:55:52.450
So you can create
an if-then statement

00:55:52.450 --> 00:55:54.340
that powers a
parameter and makes

00:55:54.340 --> 00:55:57.370
it easier for data consumers
to filter and control

00:55:57.370 --> 00:55:58.370
the dashboard.

00:55:58.370 --> 00:56:00.280
So what you see here
is the slice by example

00:56:00.280 --> 00:56:01.910
we were talking about before.

00:56:01.910 --> 00:56:05.170
So what you would do is you
would create a calculated field

00:56:05.170 --> 00:56:08.410
in Tableau called, say,
Slice By, that then

00:56:08.410 --> 00:56:10.820
points at the output
of a parameter.

00:56:10.820 --> 00:56:12.970
So that allows you
to quickly toggle

00:56:12.970 --> 00:56:15.940
a dashboard between looking
at everything by country

00:56:15.940 --> 00:56:17.922
versus looking at
everything by device.

00:56:17.922 --> 00:56:20.380
It can be really handy when
you're talking about conversion

00:56:20.380 --> 00:56:21.890
rates, for example.

00:56:21.890 --> 00:56:24.760
You might see a very
different conversion rate

00:56:24.760 --> 00:56:29.650
on mobile versus tablet versus
desktop, and the most common

00:56:29.650 --> 00:56:32.980
we have is to look at-- to jump
in and out of, say, countries

00:56:32.980 --> 00:56:33.767
and regions.

00:56:33.767 --> 00:56:34.600
JESSE NICHOLS: Yeah.

00:56:34.600 --> 00:56:36.490
Yeah, I mean this was
also-- as we started

00:56:36.490 --> 00:56:38.590
to do more of these kind
of metrics as dimensions

00:56:38.590 --> 00:56:41.920
concept, where you're
taking your underlying KPIs,

00:56:41.920 --> 00:56:45.211
the core segment of the traffic,
a couple of your core metrics,

00:56:45.211 --> 00:56:46.960
and you're building
all the visualizations

00:56:46.960 --> 00:56:47.840
you want from that.

00:56:47.840 --> 00:56:51.070
And then you use this parameter
concept to start splitting

00:56:51.070 --> 00:56:55.420
by all of the major
metrics as dimensions

00:56:55.420 --> 00:56:56.530
that you've built up.

00:56:56.530 --> 00:57:01.210
Again, finding opportunities
is far more fluid, far more

00:57:01.210 --> 00:57:03.400
natural, and also,
as you make changes

00:57:03.400 --> 00:57:05.290
to some of these
things, if you're

00:57:05.290 --> 00:57:08.110
trying to analyze the
improvement to a conversion

00:57:08.110 --> 00:57:11.200
rate after having to watch a
video or a number of videos

00:57:11.200 --> 00:57:13.540
or certain videos or engaging
with certain features

00:57:13.540 --> 00:57:16.120
on your site, it's a
very painful process

00:57:16.120 --> 00:57:17.410
of exploration in GA.

00:57:17.410 --> 00:57:19.866
But once they're
parameters in Tableau,

00:57:19.866 --> 00:57:22.240
it's just a simple matter of
flipping through and looking

00:57:22.240 --> 00:57:25.180
for what strikes you that day.

00:57:25.180 --> 00:57:27.850
ALEX BAIN: It's also, you can
build the view around whatever

00:57:27.850 --> 00:57:30.254
the goals are for
the business so that,

00:57:30.254 --> 00:57:32.170
as opposed to exposing
kind of everything that

00:57:32.170 --> 00:57:34.720
is possible to look at, you
narrow the stakeholder's

00:57:34.720 --> 00:57:36.370
field of view to
only the things that

00:57:36.370 --> 00:57:38.440
are the goals for the year.

00:57:38.440 --> 00:57:40.770
So if they're working
on conversion rate,

00:57:40.770 --> 00:57:43.540
you'd be looking at that just
by country and device as opposed

00:57:43.540 --> 00:57:46.390
to any other sort of
possible dimension

00:57:46.390 --> 00:57:49.487
that you could want
to get crazy with.

00:57:49.487 --> 00:57:50.320
JESSE NICHOLS: Cool.

00:57:50.320 --> 00:57:52.370
And finally, when using
BigQuery with Tableau,

00:57:52.370 --> 00:57:54.370
again, I assume this is
a bit of a choice you're

00:57:54.370 --> 00:57:56.828
making with most of the times
you're going to use these two

00:57:56.828 --> 00:57:58.030
tools interact.

00:57:58.030 --> 00:57:59.470
But you need to
decide whether you

00:57:59.470 --> 00:58:03.100
want a pre-aggregated table
or something closer to like

00:58:03.100 --> 00:58:05.090
a visit or session-level table.

00:58:05.090 --> 00:58:08.130
And really, this is the
tension choice between,

00:58:08.130 --> 00:58:10.420
do you want speed, or
do you want flexibility?

00:58:10.420 --> 00:58:13.210
Or in my case, are you
complacent with laziness,

00:58:13.210 --> 00:58:15.220
or do you really demand
complexity from the work

00:58:15.220 --> 00:58:16.690
that you're about to do?

00:58:16.690 --> 00:58:19.360
And really, for
pre-aggregated tables,

00:58:19.360 --> 00:58:21.700
I think the single
defining factor that's

00:58:21.700 --> 00:58:23.440
going to make it the
most obvious choice

00:58:23.440 --> 00:58:26.470
is length of time and
complexity of underlying query.

00:58:26.470 --> 00:58:28.930
If you've got a
fairly complex query,

00:58:28.930 --> 00:58:35.770
and you're trying to do analysis
over six, 12, 24, 36 months,

00:58:35.770 --> 00:58:38.050
it's best, if possible,
to simplify it

00:58:38.050 --> 00:58:40.870
into a pre-aggregated table, and
it'll be just moving lightning

00:58:40.870 --> 00:58:43.120
fast without any pause.

00:58:43.120 --> 00:58:44.999
So a lot easier to do
historical analysis.

00:58:44.999 --> 00:58:46.540
If it's a relatively
simple function,

00:58:46.540 --> 00:58:48.640
you just keep sending
it to the same table,

00:58:48.640 --> 00:58:51.155
and it's kind of
rolling on its own.

00:58:51.155 --> 00:58:53.050
Visit-level tables--
I tend to think

00:58:53.050 --> 00:58:57.490
of those more as an analyst
tool than a sharable dashboard.

00:58:57.490 --> 00:59:00.040
They tend to move a lot slower
if you have a lot of data,

00:59:00.040 --> 00:59:02.337
and they're way more
flexible, so you

00:59:02.337 --> 00:59:04.420
don't need to think about
every aggregation you're

00:59:04.420 --> 00:59:06.250
going to do up front.

00:59:06.250 --> 00:59:09.430
A classic example
is something where

00:59:09.430 --> 00:59:12.040
there are many possible
combinations of users that you

00:59:12.040 --> 00:59:13.540
would want to
explore, and you don't

00:59:13.540 --> 00:59:16.630
want to have to pre-aggregate
every possible one beforehand.

00:59:16.630 --> 00:59:21.880
So one of the cooler projects
we've done based on this

00:59:21.880 --> 00:59:24.620
technology, having a visit-level
table in BigQuery powering

00:59:24.620 --> 00:59:25.280
through--

00:59:25.280 --> 00:59:28.630
visualized through Tableau,
was a product-level funnel,

00:59:28.630 --> 00:59:31.475
starting from the earliest point
in the shopping flow, where

00:59:31.475 --> 00:59:33.100
you can engage with
that product, which

00:59:33.100 --> 00:59:35.350
is different for each
product, and then

00:59:35.350 --> 00:59:39.000
showing the fall-off as you
went through to conversion.

00:59:39.000 --> 00:59:41.369
And a single person
could and often did

00:59:41.369 --> 00:59:43.660
engage with multiple product
funnels in a single visit.

00:59:43.660 --> 00:59:46.159
So you couldn't possibly have
had an outer table that really

00:59:46.159 --> 00:59:48.940
kind of summed all that up
without having just hundreds

00:59:48.940 --> 00:59:51.880
of rows or hundreds
of lines of SQL code.

00:59:51.880 --> 00:59:53.830
And so with an inner
query and a little work

00:59:53.830 --> 00:59:56.420
in Tableau, we got to
the answers we needed,

00:59:56.420 --> 00:59:58.150
and it was just
phenomenally insightful.

00:59:58.150 --> 01:00:01.390
Not something I share with the
general public data consumers

01:00:01.390 --> 01:00:03.610
at Nest, but it was
super helpful for us

01:00:03.610 --> 01:00:06.290
growth analysts looking for
product-level opportunities.

01:00:09.580 --> 01:00:11.650
And to wrap things
up, here is just

01:00:11.650 --> 01:00:15.490
my go-to list of the dimensions
as they appear in BigQuery.

01:00:15.490 --> 01:00:19.270
They might seem a little
random or ad hoc to you guys,

01:00:19.270 --> 01:00:21.640
but out of the
200-plus dimensions

01:00:21.640 --> 01:00:24.580
that I use in
BigQuery for GA data,

01:00:24.580 --> 01:00:26.260
this represents 90% of them.

01:00:26.260 --> 01:00:30.640
And I get really, really
far out of just this stuff.

01:00:30.640 --> 01:00:32.812
So again, I can't encourage
you enough to check out

01:00:32.812 --> 01:00:34.520
that integration, if
you haven't already.

01:00:34.520 --> 01:00:36.770
It's an order of magnitude
difference in the powers

01:00:36.770 --> 01:00:38.330
you get as an analyst.

01:00:38.330 --> 01:00:40.720
ALEX BAIN: So stay
tuned for live Q&amp;A.

01:00:40.720 --> 01:00:42.220
We'll be back in
less than a minute.

01:03:29.970 --> 01:03:31.230
Welcome back.

01:03:31.230 --> 01:03:34.970
So we've got some Q&amp;A
we'll be going through now.

01:03:34.970 --> 01:03:37.370
We've got some good questions.

01:03:37.370 --> 01:03:40.130
So first question-- how do
you account for discrepancies

01:03:40.130 --> 01:03:42.350
between what you see
in Google Analytics

01:03:42.350 --> 01:03:44.340
and what you get from BigQuery?

01:03:44.340 --> 01:03:45.560
So great question.

01:03:45.560 --> 01:03:47.240
It does come up
from time to time.

01:03:47.240 --> 01:03:50.270
I can think of two sort of
classic examples of where

01:03:50.270 --> 01:03:50.960
you'd see this.

01:03:50.960 --> 01:03:54.620
So one would be if you are
looking at a long time series--

01:03:54.620 --> 01:03:57.860
for example, if you wanted
to evaluate the last three

01:03:57.860 --> 01:04:00.770
black Fridays that you'd
had some sort of campaign

01:04:00.770 --> 01:04:01.760
or promotion around.

01:04:01.760 --> 01:04:04.160
You're pulling in
three years of data.

01:04:04.160 --> 01:04:07.550
With Google Analytics,
you'd get into sampling, so

01:04:07.550 --> 01:04:09.260
at a week level, you
would start to see

01:04:09.260 --> 01:04:12.720
discrepancies between what
were the actual data--

01:04:12.720 --> 01:04:16.160
the actual, say,
visits in BigQuery

01:04:16.160 --> 01:04:20.120
versus the sampled
view of that from GA.

01:04:20.120 --> 01:04:21.732
So sampling could
be one reason why

01:04:21.732 --> 01:04:22.940
you would have a discrepancy.

01:04:22.940 --> 01:04:26.330
Another kind of classic reason
might be that you have actually

01:04:26.330 --> 01:04:30.530
structured the BigQuery
segment, for example,

01:04:30.530 --> 01:04:33.530
that you're treating as kind of
the source of truth for, say,

01:04:33.530 --> 01:04:36.710
sessions-- and maybe the
shopper example is a good one--

01:04:36.710 --> 01:04:43.400
to exclude support traffic or
to exclude traffic to forums.

01:04:43.400 --> 01:04:47.480
So it's intended to
leave certain things out,

01:04:47.480 --> 01:04:50.490
whereas GA might be
bringing in more raw data.

01:04:50.490 --> 01:04:53.297
So in that case, the
BigQuery was intended

01:04:53.297 --> 01:04:54.380
to be the source of truth.

01:04:54.380 --> 01:04:55.857
And it's often a purposeful way.

01:04:55.857 --> 01:04:57.440
JESSE NICHOLS: Yeah,
that was actually

01:04:57.440 --> 01:05:00.650
something I got a lot over the--
when I first introduced shopper

01:05:00.650 --> 01:05:04.010
traffic as a metric
is not very easily

01:05:04.010 --> 01:05:06.660
or at all discoverable within
Google Analytics, because you

01:05:06.660 --> 01:05:08.660
had to do some segmentation
magic, much of which

01:05:08.660 --> 01:05:11.182
was only available through
what I was doing in BigQuery.

01:05:11.182 --> 01:05:13.640
And so I had to kind of retrain
a lot of our data consumers

01:05:13.640 --> 01:05:15.200
as to what the
source of truth was

01:05:15.200 --> 01:05:16.907
for certain types of questions.

01:05:16.907 --> 01:05:18.740
And once it kind of
became the nomenclature,

01:05:18.740 --> 01:05:20.323
people accepted, oh,
the shopper thing

01:05:20.323 --> 01:05:22.820
is a thing we get from Tableau.

01:05:22.820 --> 01:05:26.362
And people went to the GA
interface less and less,

01:05:26.362 --> 01:05:28.195
if that's not a question
that they felt they

01:05:28.195 --> 01:05:29.319
could get the answer there.

01:05:29.319 --> 01:05:30.409
ALEX BAIN: Yes.

01:05:30.409 --> 01:05:30.950
So let's see.

01:05:30.950 --> 01:05:32.490
So the next question--

01:05:32.490 --> 01:05:36.260
what have been some of the most
insightful analysis projects

01:05:36.260 --> 01:05:39.290
using Tableau and BigQuery?

01:05:39.290 --> 01:05:41.010
So a great question.

01:05:41.010 --> 01:05:43.760
So I think GA, by
default, ends up

01:05:43.760 --> 01:05:46.820
doing a lot of last click
attribution when you're looking

01:05:46.820 --> 01:05:52.820
at marketing, so you'll get
a lot of credit for search

01:05:52.820 --> 01:05:54.170
and less so for display--

01:05:54.170 --> 01:05:56.470
typically, display advertising.

01:05:56.470 --> 01:06:00.140
What we started doing was to
fold in aggregated data out

01:06:00.140 --> 01:06:03.500
of DoubleClick, and we
had some rules set up

01:06:03.500 --> 01:06:08.130
around how to attribute revenue
back to display advertising.

01:06:08.130 --> 01:06:10.795
So we had that aggregated
and calculated,

01:06:10.795 --> 01:06:12.170
and then we would
kind of compare

01:06:12.170 --> 01:06:16.010
that to channel level
traffic and activity that we

01:06:16.010 --> 01:06:17.910
were seeing in GA.

01:06:17.910 --> 01:06:19.580
You could map one
against the other

01:06:19.580 --> 01:06:23.600
to say, OK, how is our
marketing doing holistically

01:06:23.600 --> 01:06:25.280
or at a channel level?

01:06:25.280 --> 01:06:29.150
And it gave us the freedom
to invest more actively

01:06:29.150 --> 01:06:30.137
in display advertising.

01:06:30.137 --> 01:06:30.970
JESSE NICHOLS: Yeah.

01:06:30.970 --> 01:06:33.590
Yeah, there's also one-- other
than the funnel part, Funnel 1,

01:06:33.590 --> 01:06:38.480
which was one of my earliest
proud sculptures I built,

01:06:38.480 --> 01:06:42.550
was that I worked with
a couple other analysts

01:06:42.550 --> 01:06:46.790
to form kind of a visitor
table or visitor ID table

01:06:46.790 --> 01:06:50.030
that would give us a sense of
who is a likely customer, even

01:06:50.030 --> 01:06:51.320
if they weren't logged in.

01:06:51.320 --> 01:06:56.720
We don't necessarily track any
customer identification level

01:06:56.720 --> 01:06:59.420
stuff in our GA
instance, particularly,

01:06:59.420 --> 01:07:01.670
but we do have a lot of
signals that they're customer,

01:07:01.670 --> 01:07:03.620
namely that we know if
they bought something

01:07:03.620 --> 01:07:06.140
from in a store, we can flag
that Google Analytics visitor

01:07:06.140 --> 01:07:07.490
ID as a customer.

01:07:07.490 --> 01:07:09.860
If they came from an email
that only went to customers,

01:07:09.860 --> 01:07:10.740
we know that as well.

01:07:10.740 --> 01:07:12.620
And so we kind of
built this rough table

01:07:12.620 --> 01:07:16.970
that gave us an approximation
of who our known customers were.

01:07:16.970 --> 01:07:19.820
And we could start to segment
by that data, just customer

01:07:19.820 --> 01:07:21.465
versus non-customer traffic.

01:07:21.465 --> 01:07:23.090
And also, incredibly
insightful, again,

01:07:23.090 --> 01:07:24.990
when you have no other way,
especially other than maybe

01:07:24.990 --> 01:07:27.650
whether they're logged in or not
as to whether that person was

01:07:27.650 --> 01:07:30.020
a customer, and huge
growth opportunities

01:07:30.020 --> 01:07:31.252
were found through that.

01:07:31.252 --> 01:07:32.191
ALEX BAIN: Yeah.

01:07:32.191 --> 01:07:32.690
Let's see.

01:07:32.690 --> 01:07:36.410
Next question-- can you get
real-time Google Analytics data

01:07:36.410 --> 01:07:38.780
through BigQuery?

01:07:38.780 --> 01:07:40.680
JESSE NICHOLS: Much
to Alex's disbelief,

01:07:40.680 --> 01:07:42.000
you actually can get it.

01:07:42.000 --> 01:07:42.190
ALEX BAIN: I didn't know.

01:07:42.190 --> 01:07:43.940
JESSE NICHOLS: And I think it
was launched sometime around

01:07:43.940 --> 01:07:44.600
last year.

01:07:44.600 --> 01:07:47.812
Yeah, with real time, it was one
of the kind of last remaining

01:07:47.812 --> 01:07:50.270
vestiges and benefits of going
through the Google Analytics

01:07:50.270 --> 01:07:51.380
interface.

01:07:51.380 --> 01:07:54.260
But getting it into
the BigQuery data set,

01:07:54.260 --> 01:07:56.230
and then piping
it over to Tableau

01:07:56.230 --> 01:07:58.400
has been really phenomenal
for our dev teams who

01:07:58.400 --> 01:08:01.904
do a lot of uptime monitoring,
just kind of like day

01:08:01.904 --> 01:08:02.570
to day check in.

01:08:02.570 --> 01:08:05.000
They've obviously got lots
of other uptime tools,

01:08:05.000 --> 01:08:10.080
but they'll blend those together
and watch QPS or, rather,

01:08:10.080 --> 01:08:13.640
more QPM, queries per minute,
site traffic per minute,

01:08:13.640 --> 01:08:15.060
and just general volume.

01:08:15.060 --> 01:08:16.660
We also use it
around launch dates,

01:08:16.660 --> 01:08:18.630
where we're pulling in
lots of real-time data--

01:08:18.630 --> 01:08:19.310
ALEX BAIN: Yeah, [INAUDIBLE].

01:08:19.310 --> 01:08:20.893
JESSE NICHOLS: --as
fresh as possible.

01:08:20.893 --> 01:08:22.609
And being about 15
minutes fresh gives us

01:08:22.609 --> 01:08:24.770
the impact of individual
press releases

01:08:24.770 --> 01:08:27.170
or individual cycles
that we put out there.

01:08:27.170 --> 01:08:29.670
And yeah, real-time
data through BigQuery

01:08:29.670 --> 01:08:32.530
has been just lightning
hot when it's needed.

01:08:32.530 --> 01:08:33.529
ALEX BAIN: So let's see.

01:08:33.529 --> 01:08:36.560
Next question-- what's the
advantage of using Tableau

01:08:36.560 --> 01:08:38.390
over Google's data vis product?

01:08:38.390 --> 01:08:38.960
JESSE NICHOLS: Yeah,
I think they're

01:08:38.960 --> 01:08:40.584
talking about Google
Data Studio, which

01:08:40.584 --> 01:08:42.762
I've had a lot of
experience with as well.

01:08:42.762 --> 01:08:43.970
Both are phenomenal products.

01:08:43.970 --> 01:08:45.979
Both have a connection
to BigQuery.

01:08:45.979 --> 01:08:48.319
Tableau has a few
advantages when

01:08:48.319 --> 01:08:52.010
you're doing some of the
more complex dashboard

01:08:52.010 --> 01:08:55.069
development or the
analysis that we

01:08:55.069 --> 01:08:57.890
talk a lot about, the
parameterizing dimensions

01:08:57.890 --> 01:09:00.790
and slice by and some
instances of color by.

01:09:00.790 --> 01:09:02.450
You just have more control.

01:09:02.450 --> 01:09:04.370
Google's Data Studio--

01:09:04.370 --> 01:09:06.796
I love it that you don't--
you have a single canvas,

01:09:06.796 --> 01:09:09.170
and you're kind of painting
the elements of the dashboard

01:09:09.170 --> 01:09:09.669
on there.

01:09:09.669 --> 01:09:13.010
So you don't need the complexity
of, like, one sheet per graph,

01:09:13.010 --> 01:09:14.259
like you need with Tableau.

01:09:14.259 --> 01:09:16.550
You can just kind of work
from one or two canvas spaces

01:09:16.550 --> 01:09:18.529
and just mix and
match as you need.

01:09:18.529 --> 01:09:20.810
So they're also just the
same as Tableau and GA,

01:09:20.810 --> 01:09:21.710
kind of situational.

01:09:21.710 --> 01:09:23.229
But both have a
BigQuery connection,

01:09:23.229 --> 01:09:24.500
so I've used them both.

01:09:24.500 --> 01:09:25.880
ALEX BAIN: Terrific.

01:09:25.880 --> 01:09:28.500
Well, that's all for today.

01:09:28.500 --> 01:09:32.060
Please tune in next Tuesday
for the next Cloud OnAir.

01:09:32.060 --> 01:09:34.460
Thank you for watching.

01:09:34.460 --> 01:09:38.410
[MUSIC PLAYING]

